From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0241] diag: name a work-loop thread once, not on every wakeup

threadMain() is not entered once per loop. The body calls itself through its
own continuation (thread_block_parameter()), so every wakeup - every round of
any event source on that loop - runs the prologue again, and the
"SWEET IOWORKLOOP: thread %p running loop %p" line printed there cost one
console row per round. On the 0134 pull 9030 of 12863 decoded rows were that
line, 70% of the 4 MiB ring, so a ring that should hold half an hour held
minutes and the boot-time SWEET CLOCK rows - the oldest ones - were the first
thing lost. It was not free even where it did not dominate: two of those rows
were recorded spliced onto the end of unrelated messages
("... serializeVariables failed, ret=0xe00002d8SWEET IOWORKLOOP: ..."), which
is one console write landing inside another message.

The line itself has to stay: a recursive-lock panic names a thread, and this is
the only thing that maps that thread to a loop. What has to change is the rate.
One row per owning thread answers the same question:

  * The bit lives in the byte fFlags already occupies. loopRestart is a bool and
    kLoopRestart/kLoopTerminate are already tested through the byte-wide
    ISSETP/SETP/CLRP helpers, so kLoopThreadNamed = 0x4 is a third bit in that
    byte: no new member, no layout change, no shared cache line. The cost is one
    read-modify-write per loop entry, which is what the two state bits cost.

  * It is per object, not one remembered thread. A single static
    "last named thread" word is defeated by alternation: with two hot loops
    alternating wakeups the word flips every time and both loops reprint on
    every wakeup, which is exactly the 4493/4485 pair in the 0134 ring. The
    shape is preserved in the test as a mutation that must be rejected.

  * It is cleared at exitThread. A loop whose thread exits and is replaced would
    otherwise stay silent, and the panic line would name an owner that is gone;
    the replacement prints once and then goes quiet too.

tools/tests/test-mh2lm-workloop-log.py compiles the shipped helpers, enum,
threadMain() prologue and exitThread cleanup verbatim into a host program and
drives them the way the device does: twelve loops round-robin for 500 wakeups
each print exactly twelve rows with the right owners, the two hot loops
alternating 4000 times print nothing, a handover prints once then stops, and a
never-named loop still prints. Four mutations are rejected - unguarded (the
0134 code), guarding the wrong bit, no cleanup, and the remembered-thread word
(caught by the alternation case).

diff --git a/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/IOWorkLoop.cpp b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/IOWorkLoop.cpp
index 614a8b4..d160498 100644
--- a/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/IOWorkLoop.cpp
+++ b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/IOWorkLoop.cpp
@@ -57,7 +57,23 @@ OSMetaClassDefineReservedUnused(IOWorkLoop, 5);
 OSMetaClassDefineReservedUnused(IOWorkLoop, 6);
 OSMetaClassDefineReservedUnused(IOWorkLoop, 7);
 
-enum IOWorkLoopState { kLoopRestart = 0x1, kLoopTerminate = 0x2 };
+/* kLoopThreadNamed is a diagnostic bit, not a loop state: threadMain() is not
+ * entered once per loop - it is re-entered through its own continuation (the
+ * thread_block_parameter() call in the body) on every wakeup - so the
+ * "thread %p running loop %p" line below would otherwise cost one console row
+ * per event-source round.  On the 0134 pull that line was 9042 of 12863 rows,
+ * 70% of the 4 MiB ring, which is why a ring that should hold half an hour
+ * held minutes.  The line exists so a recursive-lock panic names a loop owner,
+ * and one line per owning thread answers that: the bit lives in the same byte
+ * as the two state bits (loopRestart is a bool and ISSETP/SETP/CLRP already
+ * test it as a byte), it is set on the loop's own object so twelve loops each
+ * print once instead of sharing one remembered thread, and it is cleared when
+ * the loop thread exits so a loop that gains a new owner announces it again. */
+enum IOWorkLoopState {
+	kLoopRestart = 0x1,
+	kLoopTerminate = 0x2,
+	kLoopThreadNamed = 0x4
+};
 static inline void
 SETP(void *addr, unsigned int flag)
 {
@@ -435,9 +451,13 @@ IOWorkLoop::threadMain()
 #ifdef SWEET
 	/* The recursive-lock panic names a thread; printing the loop's own
 	 * thread here and the touch worker's in ts_thread() lets the panic line
-	 * be matched against a known owner instead of an anonymous pointer. */
-	printf("SWEET IOWORKLOOP: thread %p running loop %p\n",
-	    (void *)IOThreadSelf(), this);
+	 * be matched against a known owner instead of an anonymous pointer.
+	 * Once per owning thread, not once per wakeup - see kLoopThreadNamed. */
+	if (!ISSETP(&fFlags, kLoopThreadNamed)) {
+		SETP(&fFlags, kLoopThreadNamed);
+		printf("SWEET IOWORKLOOP: thread %p running loop %p\n",
+		    (void *)IOThreadSelf(), this);
+	}
 #endif
 restartThread:
 	do {
@@ -466,6 +486,11 @@ restartThread:
 	} while (workToDo);
 
 exitThread:
+#ifdef SWEET
+	/* This loop is about to lose the thread the line above named; let a
+	 * replacement thread announce itself instead of inheriting the bit. */
+	CLRP(&fFlags, kLoopThreadNamed);
+#endif
 	closeGate();
 	thread_t thread = workThread;
 	workThread = NULL; // Say we don't have a loop and free ourselves
